# The map of the frozen records — §4.9.4. ⛔ One check and not two: a separate index registry
# would be a second place to keep aligned for the same property, and the first one to stop
# being updated lies in silence (§7.4.4). This map lives INSIDE the artefact it describes.
#
# Produced from `the_frozen_records()` in ../frozen_bytes.rs, by hand, from the hexadecimal
# output of a throwaway probe deleted in the commit that added its file — the first three
# on 2026-08-10, the verdict on 2026-08-31. Each file, its own probe.
# ⛔ NOT REGENERATED. If the bytes change, a new VERSION of the record is opened.
#
# ⛔ ONE MAP FOR ALL THE FILES, AND THE ASYMMETRY IS DELIBERATE. The `.cbor` files are separate
# because each one is an irreproducible artefact and a single blob would make a one-record
# change look like a whole-archive change; the map is one because it describes ONE format, and
# a map per file would be one place per file to keep aligned for a single property — the very
# thing the paragraph above refuses.
# ⚠️ RECALL OF 2026-08-31: this line said "FOR THREE FILES" and the map had four sections by
# then. Taken out and not realigned — how many files there are is the sections below, and the
# bench reads them (errata `E70`).
#
# ⛔ WHAT IS CHECKED HERE AND WHAT IS NOT — read this before believing a cell.
#   Rows are `offset | hex bytes | prose`, and a line that is only a file name opens a section.
#   ✅ The OFFSET and the HEX columns are CHECKED, by `the_map_lists_the_bytes_that_are_really_
#      frozen` in ../frozen_bytes.rs: every row is read back, the offsets must be contiguous,
#      and the bytes must rebuild the `.cbor` file exactly. A placeholder left in the hex column
#      does not parse, and a byte typed wrong does not match.
#   ✅ The claim that byte 4 is `kind`, byte 5 `effect`, byte 6 `trust`, 7..14 the payload and
#      14..21 the reason is CHECKED too, by `every_field_sits_at_the_offset_the_map_gives_it`,
#      which changes one field at a time and demands that the encoding move there and nowhere
#      else.
#   ⚠️ The rest of the PROSE column is PROSE — unverified. Nothing reads the words `array(2)`
#      or `EffectClass::Idempotent`. It is said out loud because a document half checked and
#      not saying which half is worse than one nobody checks at all.
#
# ⚠️ THE FRAMING IS FOUR BYTES, NOT THREE, and it is the thing the obvious reading gets wrong:
# `82 00` is the version enum and its variant index, then `81` — the ONE-ELEMENT ARRAY of the
# variant's body — and only then `85`, the five fields. So the fields begin at byte 4.
#
# ⛔ RECORDS OF THE SAME ARITY DIFFER ONLY AT BYTES 4, 5 AND 6, on purpose: a record pins ONE
# variant of each `index_only` enum, so a single record would leave every other variant of them
# held by nothing, and the set has to cover them all. Everything else about those records is
# identical, so the difference between two files of one arity is exactly the enum bytes.
# ⛔ A RECORD CARRYING A `detail` IS LONGER AND ALSO MOVES BYTE 3, the field-array header —
# so it differs from the others outside 4..7 by construction, and the bench
# compares the WHERE only within one arity.
# ⚠️ RECALL OF 2026-08-31: this paragraph said "THE THREE RECORDS", "EIGHT variants —
# RecordKind 3", "Three is the fewest" and "the difference between any two files"; all four
# went false when `Verdict` and index 5 arrived. Taken out and not realigned to four and nine,
# because both grow with the format (errata `E70`).
#
# ⚠️ `payload` AND `reason` CARRY THE SAME SIX CHARACTERS, so the two major types sit side by
# side in the bytes: `46` is a byte string of 6, `66` is text of 6. One nibble apart, and the
# asymmetry is in the archive instead of only in the source.

record_v1_intent.cbor
  0 | 82                   | array(2) — the version enum
  1 | 00                   | variant index 0 = Record::V1
  2 | 81                   | array(1) — the body of the variant
  3 | 85                   | array(5) — the five fields of RecordV1
  4 | 00                   | index 0 · kind    · RecordKind::Intent
  5 | 01                   | index 1 · effect  · EffectClass::Idempotent
  6 | 01                   | index 2 · trust   · Trust::Untrusted
  7 | 46 66 72 6f 7a 65 6e | index 3 · payload · byte string(6) b"frozen"
 14 | 66 66 72 6f 7a 65 6e | index 4 · reason  · text(6) "frozen"

